connection_kernel — implementation plan
=======================================

The current serve loop combines stdin, JSON-RPC interpretation, application
dispatch, concurrency, completion, and shutdown in one object.  That makes a
transport change, a protocol-revision change, and a request-ownership fix all
touch the same code.  It also leaves the existing one-thread-per-request
implementation without a bound and makes the EOF drain regression difficult to
repair without changing unrelated behavior.

goal
----

Extract a small connection kernel whose state transitions and ownership rules
can be tested without Isabelle.  Keep byte transport, MCP connection control,
and Isabelle tool behavior separate and replaceable:

    composition root
      -> DataPlane
      -> ConnectionKernel(RevisionRules, RequestScheduler, policy)
      -> McpApplication
      -> Isabelle backend

This is an internal architecture change.  Stdio remains supported, the server
remains single-client, and MCP 2025-03-26 remains the selected revision.

boundaries
----------

The data plane converts a byte stream into typed JSON-RPC envelopes and writes
complete encoded envelopes atomically.  The first implementations are
`StdioDataPlane` for production and `ScriptedDataPlane` for deterministic
tests.  It does not know lifecycle phases, request limits, tools, or Isabelle.
HTTP is not part of this work package and would be another adapter rather than
a replacement for stdio.

The connection kernel owns:

- MCP lifecycle and revision validation;
- request admission, identities, deadlines, and terminal dispositions;
- cancellation races and completion-token validation;
- batch response aggregation;
- bounded shutdown drain and connection health; and
- serialization of outbound writes through the data-plane contract.

The application maps an accepted MCP operation to tools, resources, and
Isabelle behavior.  It sees an application request and cancellation handle,
but never a JSON-RPC request ID, output stream, lifecycle state, scheduler, or
completion token.  Isabelle `Not_Ready`, `Ready`, and `Failed` remain an
application state machine independent from the MCP connection phases.

Scala packages and visibility provide the compile-time enforcement needed for
this work package.  Protocol values, connection-owned token constructors, and
registry mutation APIs are package-private to their owner; consumers receive
only narrow traits and opaque handles.  The composition root is the only code
that can construct and connect concrete adapters.  No bytecode dependency
checker or separate JAR is introduced.

initial source layout
---------------------

The first extraction uses small Scala compilation units rather than adding more
responsibilities to `mcp_server.scala`:

    mcp_protocol.scala           typed JSON-RPC values and 2025-03-26 rules
    mcp_data_plane.scala         DataPlane, StdioDataPlane, ScriptedDataPlane
    mcp_connection_policy.scala validated policy and option composition
    mcp_request_scheduler.scala bounded and deterministic schedulers
    mcp_connection_kernel.scala lifecycle, registry, batches, and shutdown
    mcp_application.scala        neutral application port and Isabelle adapter
    mcp_server.scala             catalog/handler code pending later extraction
    mcp-main.scala               composition root

The protocol, connection, and application types use corresponding nested Scala
packages below `isabelle.mcp`.  A private constructor plus a public factory
lets the composition root create a kernel while keeping registry mutation and
completion-token construction inaccessible to adapters.

connection policy
-----------------

The composition root reads Isabelle options once and constructs one validated
value for each connection:

    ConnectionPolicy(
      revision = ProtocolRevision.v2025_03_26,
      admission = AdmissionPolicy(
        maxInFlight = MaxInFlight.checked(...)),
      timing = TimingPolicy(
        requestTimeout = RequestTimeout.checked(...),
        shutdownDrain = ShutdownDrain.checked(...)))

Correctness-affecting fields have no defaults.  Named arguments make additions
review-visible at construction sites; nested policy values keep unrelated
fields from accumulating in one flat parameter list; opaque or smart-created
leaf values reject invalid numbers before a connection begins.  A future field
is added to the smallest owning policy and deliberately supplied by the single
options-to-policy function.

The initial concurrent scheduler has a hard `maxInFlight` and no application
waiting queue.  Admission is a non-blocking attempt: either the request owns a
slot or it receives an immediate structured overload error.  A direct-handoff
bounded executor is suitable for production.  The deterministic scheduler
executes sequentially or under explicit test control, allowing exact replay of
state transitions without changing the kernel.

control path and application work
---------------------------------

Control-path operations change or inspect connection ownership and therefore
run inline in the kernel: initialize/initialized, ping, cancellation,
admission, completion bookkeeping, and shutdown.  They do not consume an
application worker slot.  This is an implementation choice that preserves
liveness under saturation, not an MCP requirement that such messages use a
particular thread.

Accepted tool and resource operations run through the scheduler and may finish
out of order.  Clients that require ordering await the earlier response.  The
kernel does not create a request-ordering queue.

lifecycle and terminal ownership
--------------------------------

The protocol phases are:

    Fresh -> Initializing -> AwaitingInitialized -> Ready -> Closing -> Closed

Fresh accepts only a standalone initialize request.  Initialize is never valid
inside a batch.  AwaitingInitialized accepts the initialized notification and
ping; ordinary operations begin in Ready.  Closing and Closed admit no new
application work.

An admitted request receives an unforgeable connection-owned completion token.
Exactly one transition wins its registry entry.  Cooperative cancellation is
propagated first through the existing Scala and Isabelle/ML cancellation
machinery.  If client cancellation wins the registry race, no response is
emitted.  A worker that cannot stop may finish internally, but its late result
is discarded.  The first late result following cancellation, timeout, or
shutdown is an expected stale completion; a second completion attempt by the
same worker or an unknown token is an invariant violation.

Detection of invariant violations is not configurable.  The reaction is.  The
connection kernel reports a structured violation plus an inspectable registry
snapshot to an injected control-plane policy:

- `FailFast` throws immediately for focused tests;
- `LogAndClose` records the violation on stderr and begins controlled shutdown;
- `MarkBroken` retains the connection in a broken state for diagnostic tests,
  rejects ordinary admissions, and exposes the snapshot to direct debug tools.

The application does not decide or observe this policy.  The composition root
selects it, allowing production and diagnostic commands to choose different
reactions without weakening detection or permitting a second response.

MCP 2025-03-26 request IDs are typed connection values, but this server-boundary
contract accepts strings only. `null` and every numeric value, including a
finite integral safe integer, are InvalidRequest values and are rejected before
admission. No precision-preserving numeric parser or safe-integer exception is
present. Python/e2e clients generate string IDs. This is a deliberate
compatibility limitation from MCP 2025-03-26's unrestricted integer domain.
The registry retains every received valid string ID for the whole connection,
including rejected requests and terminal tombstones, and rejects per-session
ID reuse before lifecycle admission; completion tokens therefore remain
independent of client IDs and cannot be redirected by reuse.

batch conformance
-----------------

Batching is implemented only because MCP 2025-03-26 requires receivers to
accept JSON-RPC batches.  The data plane decodes a single envelope or a batch;
revision rules reject an empty batch and any batch containing initialize.  Each
request element passes through ordinary admission, so a batch cannot bypass
`maxInFlight`; notification elements do not consume response slots.

A connection-owned batch context waits for every response-producing element to
reach a terminal disposition, omits notifications and client-cancelled
elements, and emits one response array.  A notification-only batch emits no
response.  Element work may run concurrently and response order is not an
application ordering guarantee.  No server feature or application API is
allowed to depend on batching.

implementation sequence and checkpoints
---------------------------------------

1. Fold the four open spec refinements and add characterization fixtures for
   current lifecycle, framing, request IDs, cancellation, concurrency, and EOF
   behavior.  The new desired counterexamples remain red where behavior is
   known to be wrong.
2. Extract typed protocol values plus stdio and scripted data planes without
   changing dispatch behavior.  Checkpoint when the existing functional suite
   is unchanged apart from named characterization failures.
3. Introduce `McpApplication` and move Isabelle/backend dispatch behind it.
   Enforce visibility boundaries and compile before changing scheduling.
4. Add validated policy values, revision rules, the lifecycle machine, and the
   deterministic scheduler.  Make the lifecycle transition table green.
5. Add the request registry, completion tokens, cooperative cancellation, and
   invariant-reaction policies.  Make terminal-disposition and race fixtures
   green before enabling concurrent execution.
6. Replace one-thread-per-request execution with the bounded direct-handoff
   scheduler and immediate overload responses.  Keep functional and
   performance tests separate.
7. Add batch aggregation behind the same admission and terminal machinery.
   Batch conformance must not introduce a second dispatch path.
8. Repair EOF shutdown around the registry: close admission, use one drain
   deadline, cancel remaining work, then tear down the backend.  Preserve every
   reply that completes before the deadline.
9. Add procedural e2e cases for lifecycle, cancellation, saturation, batching,
   backend readiness, and EOF drain using the registered Python infrastructure.
10. Run `tools/planning-gate done`.  Do not change this plan to done while any
    claim lacks all named test links, any refinement remains open, or any
    functional layer is red.

Each numbered checkpoint should be a small reviewable commit after its focused
tests pass.  Findings that change client-visible guarantees go back through
spec refinement before the implementation proceeds; internal ownership details
may update this plan directly.

non-goals
---------

- Moving to a newer MCP revision or introducing revision selection at runtime.
- Replacing or removing stdio transport.
- Serving multiple clients from one Isabelle application instance.
- Dynamically reconfiguring a live connection through an administrative API.
- Adding an application request queue or guaranteeing request completion order.
- Treating performance thresholds as functional correctness assertions.
